### Description

In a `module`, I have defined two instances with identical signature, first `private`, then without modifier. Instead of getting an error that the declaration already exists, the elaborator panics because the automatically chosen names clash.

### Context

This occurred when I was porting some code to the module system.

### Steps to Reproduce

Run this on commit `0002ea` which is the latest master (2025-06-25; can't use Lean4Web with the module system and wasn't able to define a `module` in an external lean project):
    
    module
    
    prelude
    import Init.Core
    
    class A
    
    private instance : A := sorry
    
    /-
    PANIC at _private.Lean.Environment.0.Lean.AsyncConsts.add Lean.Environment:432:4: duplicate normalized declaration name instA vs. _private.Init.Data.Test.0.instA
    -/
    instance : A := sorry

Expected behavior:

no panic and a helpful error instead.

Actual behavior:

panic, see above

### Versions
    
    `Lean 4.22.0, commit 
    Target: unknown
    `

I do not think this output is super helpful because I am using the `lean4` development setup on the latest master (commit `0002ea`), though.

### Additional Information

See also #8627, which seems similar but not identical because it is unrelated to the module system.

