citeturn33search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; The useful audit artifact is the final theorem signature, its assumptions and dependency graph, and the clause-by-clause mapping to the human ...
Treat this as a refinement chain, not one proof obligation. Start with a requirement table that maps each clause in the Clay statement to the exact Lean declaration or assumption. Reviewers should flag every place where the formal statement is weaker, stronger, or simply uses a different definition. For this problem, the bridge lemmas around regularity, domain, forcing class, energy bounds, and the meaning of singularity deserve as much scrutiny as the proof built on top of them.

A stronger check would use two independent formalizations. One group encodes the official problem; another encodes the claimed result. Then prove the relevant implication or equivalence between those statements. Having the same model translate both the specification and the proof leaves both sides exposed to the same mistranslation.

The useful audit artifact is the final theorem signature, its assumptions and dependency graph, and the clause-by-clause mapping to the human statement. A green Lean check certifies the derivation inside the chosen definitions. It does not certify the sentence used to describe it.

